-- title:
--   Normal form n=n₀z for a level-Nq Eichler idele
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb Q$ and let $q'$ be a prime such that $\mathbb H=\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt`, i.e. $a<0$, $b<0$, and for every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q_v$ has all nonzero elements invertible exactly when $q'$ lies in $v$. Let $\Lambda_1\subseteq\mathbb H$ be a $\mathbb Z$-submodule which is a maximal order (an order: it contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb H$ over $\mathbb Q$ and is finitely generated; and maximal among such), let $N\neq 0$ be a natural number with $q'\nmid N$, and let $m$ be a unit of $\mathbb H\otimes_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ such that $m$ and $N\cdot m^{-1}$ lie in the adelic box $\widehat\Lambda_1$ of $\Lambda_1$ (the additive group generated by the $z\otimes\alpha$ with $z\in\Lambda_1$ and $\alpha$ an integral finite adele) and such that the conjugate order [`Submodule.conjByFiniteIdele`](def/Submodule_FiniteAdeleBox.html#L31) $\Lambda_1\,m$, namely the submodule of $x\in\mathbb H$ with $x\otimes 1\in m\widehat\Lambda_1m^{-1}$, is again maximal. Put $R=\Lambda_1\sqcap m\widehat\Lambda_1m^{-1}\cap\mathbb H$ and assume the relative index of $R$ in $\Lambda_1$ (as additive groups) is $N$. Let $q\neq q'$ be a prime with $q\nmid N$, and let $n$ be a finite idele such that $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R\,n=R\sqcap(\text{conjugate of }R\text{ by }n)$ is an Eichler order of level $Nq$, that is, an intersection $\Lambda\sqcap\Lambda'$ of two maximal orders of relative index $Nq$ in $\Lambda$. Then $n$ factors as $n=n_0z$ with $n_0$ in the prime Hecke set $\mathrm{primeHeckeSet}\,\Lambda_1\,q$ (i.e. $n_0\in\widehat\Lambda_1$, $q\cdot n_0^{-1}\in\widehat\Lambda_1$, $n_0^{-1}\notin\widehat\Lambda_1$ and $q^{-1}\cdot n_0\notin\widehat\Lambda_1$), with $z$ normalising the stabiliser $\mathrm{finiteIdeleStabilizer}\,R$ of $\widehat R$ in the sense that for every idele $u$ one has $u$ in that stabiliser if and only if $zuz^{-1}$ is, and with $m^{-1}n_0m\in\widehat\Lambda_1$, $n_0m\in\widehat\Lambda_1$, $Nq\cdot(n_0m)^{-1}\in\widehat\Lambda_1$, the conjugate order of $\Lambda_1$ by $n_0m$ maximal, $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R\,n=\Lambda_1\sqcap(n_0m)\widehat\Lambda_1(n_0m)^{-1}\cap\mathbb H$, and the relative index of $\mathrm{meetOrder}\,R\,n$ in $\Lambda_1$ equal to $Nq$.
--
--   This is the quaternionic bookkeeping step which shows that, after splitting off the part of $n$ normalising $\widehat R$ (Atkin–Lehner elements at primes dividing $N$ and the uniformiser at the ramified place $q'$), the maximal order $\Lambda_1$ is still one of the two maximal orders cutting out the level-$Nq$ Eichler order, with the second one given by the explicitly normalised idele $n_0m$ and $n_0$ a degree-$q$ Hecke idele. It is used in [`CerednikDrinfeld.exists_equiv_classSet_ssPlaces_degeneracy_hecke_comm`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.exists_equiv_classSet_ssPlaces_degeneracy_hecke_comm), where the normaliser factor $z$ accounts for the permutation occurring in the compatibility of the degeneracy maps with the Hecke correspondence at $q$.
